{
  "policy_id": "ref-57-prepare-for-renegotiations-on-the-eu",
  "party": "Reform UK",
  "policy_title": "Prepare for renegotiations on the EU Trade and Cooperation Agreement",
  "outcome": "O13",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O13",
    "direction": "too-uncertain",
    "magnitude": "n/a",
    "time_horizon": "n/a",
    "confidence": "low",
    "plain": "This policy only commits to 'preparing' for renegotiations \u2014 there is no guaranteed outcome, and whether any actual renegotiation would help or hurt living standards depends entirely on what terms emerge. The evidence suggests the EU would strongly resist weakening the level-playing-field rules, and that regulatory divergence so far has yielded limited benefits while increased trade barriers have already hurt productivity.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether the EU would agree to any meaningful weakening of LPF provisions, or instead trigger the TCA's rebalancing mechanism imposing new tariffs on UK goods, is entirely unresolved \u2014 the direction of effect on living standards flips depending on that answer.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy commits to preparing for renegotiations on the TCA, characterising the level-playing-field provisions as holding Britain back.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Reform UK will prepare for renegotiations on the EU Trade and Cooperation Agreement, stating that a 'level playing field' is holding Britain back."
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The OBR projects the post-Brexit TCA trading relationship will reduce long-run UK productivity by 4% relative to remaining in the EU.",
        "evidence_id": "E10",
        "quote": "The OBR forecasts that the post-Brexit trading relationship, as set out in the TCA, will reduce long-run UK productivity by 4% relative to remaining in the EU, largely due to increased non-tariff barriers"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "UK goods exports to the EU were 14% below their 2019 real-terms level as of 2025.",
        "evidence_id": "E12",
        "quote": "In 2025, UK goods exports to the EU were 14% below their 2019 real-terms level"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Any attempt to significantly weaken LPF provisions would likely face strong EU resistance and potential retaliatory measures.",
        "evidence_id": "E34",
        "quote": "Any attempt to significantly weaken these provisions would likely be met with strong resistance and potential retaliatory measures from the EU"
      }
    ],
    "uncertainty": {
      "is_genuine": true,
      "crux_parameter": "Whether renegotiations result in meaningful LPF relaxation accepted by the EU without triggering rebalancing tariffs, or instead provoke EU countermeasures that further restrict goods trade",
      "estimate_range": "If EU imposes rebalancing tariffs, further productivity drag compounds the already-projected 4% OBR reduction; if divergence yields new competitive advantages, Resolution Foundation and UK in a Changing Europe evidence suggests gains historically have been small and have not offset trade-barrier costs"
    },
    "rationale": "The policy as stated is purely preparatory \u2014 'prepare for renegotiations' involves no committed instrument, no quantified target, and no guaranteed outcome. Under the soft-verb rule alone, this would score negligible. However, the aspiration is directionally clear: weakening or removing LPF provisions. The genuine uncertainty arises at the next level: what would actually happen if renegotiations were pursued in earnest?\n\nThe evidence points in two opposed directions. On the downside: the OBR projects a 4% long-run productivity loss from the current TCA, and UK goods exports are already 14% below 2019 levels. If renegotiations triggered EU rebalancing countermeasures, this would compound existing trade damage \u2014 directly harming business investment and real living standards. The institutional consensus is that the EU views LPF as foundational and would resist weakening it. On the upside: proponents argue regulatory freedom could unlock dynamism, but Resolution Foundation and UK in a Changing Europe find divergence benefits to date have been limited and outweighed by integration costs.\n\nThe policy does not reach a renegotiation outcome \u2014 it only 'prepares'. The actual effect on O13 is entirely contingent on: (a) whether renegotiations are formally triggered; (b) what the EU agrees to; (c) whether greater regulatory freedom materialises in productive investment gains; and (d) whether countermeasures are imposed. No provided evidence resolves this chain. The crux \u2014 EU acceptance vs. retaliation \u2014 spans outcomes from modest productivity gain to accelerated trade deterioration. This is genuine uncertainty: credible analysts disagree on mechanism and magnitude, and the deciding parameter is unobservable in advance. 'Too-uncertain' is therefore the honest verdict.",
